Flutter Folder Architecture
Purpose

The purpose of the Flutter folder architecture is to establish a scalable, maintainable, and organized project structure before development begins. As Predictive Planner is expected to grow from a simple MVP into a full-featured adaptive scheduling platform, the architecture must support future expansion without requiring significant restructuring.

The chosen architecture emphasizes modularity, separation of concerns, and feature independence while remaining understandable for a solo developer learning Flutter.

Architecture Goals

The Flutter architecture was designed with the following primary objectives:

Scalability – Support future features such as cloud synchronization, machine learning, and automatic scheduling without major architectural changes.
Faster Feature Development – Keep all code related to a specific feature together so development and future enhancements are more efficient.
Readability – Organize the codebase so that it is intuitive to navigate and understand, reducing development time and improving maintainability.

As the application grows, new developers—or the original developer returning months later—should be able to quickly locate all code associated with a particular feature.

Architectural Approach

Predictive Planner will use a Feature-Based Clean Architecture.

Rather than organizing files by type (such as placing all screens, models, or providers into separate top-level folders), the project will organize code by feature. Each feature contains its own presentation, business logic, and data access layers.

This approach allows each feature to evolve independently and minimizes the impact of future changes.

Why Feature-Based Organization

A traditional folder structure groups files by type:

screens/
models/
providers/
widgets/

As applications grow, related files become scattered throughout the project, making maintenance increasingly difficult.

Predictive Planner instead groups files by feature:

features/
    tasks/
    calendar/
    prediction/
    analytics/

This organization keeps all functionality related to a feature in one location, making it easier to:

Develop new functionality
Debug issues
Update existing features
Understand the codebase
Clean Architecture Strategy

Clean Architecture separates the application into independent layers, ensuring each layer has a single responsibility.

This separation allows individual components of the system to be modified without affecting unrelated parts of the application.

For example, if the statistical prediction algorithm is replaced with a neural network in a future release, only the Prediction feature should require significant modification while the user interface, task management system, and calendar remain unchanged.

Data Flow

The application will follow a centralized data flow.

User Creates Task
        ↓
Task Saved
        ↓
Calendar Updated
        ↓
Prediction Updated
        ↓
Analytics Updated

This flow ensures that every completed task contributes to the adaptive learning system while maintaining a single source of truth for application data.

Database Independence

The architecture is designed to isolate data storage from the rest of the application.

The MVP will use a local SQLite database through Drift. Future versions will introduce Supabase and PostgreSQL for cloud synchronization.

Because database operations are isolated within the data layer, migrating from local storage to cloud storage should require minimal changes outside the data layer.

This design reduces technical debt and supports long-term scalability.

Future Expansion

The selected architecture is intended to support future development without requiring major reorganization.

Planned future modules include:

Authentication
Cloud Synchronization
Google Calendar Integration
Notifications
Automatic Scheduling
Machine Learning
Goal Tracking

Each feature can be added as its own module while preserving the existing project structure.

Learning Considerations

One objective of this project is to develop a deeper understanding of Flutter software architecture and engineering best practices.

Although a simplified architecture would allow faster initial development, Predictive Planner will adopt a Feature-Based Clean Architecture from the beginning. This approach provides valuable experience with professional software engineering practices while creating a maintainable foundation for future development.

As the developer gains experience with Flutter, the existing architecture will support continued expansion without requiring significant refactoring.

